<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.huffmanwrites.weekly-integrity</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>/Users/prh/Developer/huffmanwrites/scripts/weekly-integrity-check.sh</string>
  </array>
  <!-- Two fires a week, and both earn their place. Until 2026-10-10 this job ran
       only on Monday 14:00 CT, an hour after the site audit at 13:00 and the wiki
       check at 13:30 so the three do not contend and a failure here is read
       alongside whatever the audit already reported. One fire was not enough once
       the vault-currency gate joined the list: a post published on a Tuesday and
       missed by the publishing runner's mirror then sat invisible for six days,
       and this sweep is the only thing in either repo that looks at the vault.
       Friday 21:00 CT halves the worst case and catches the week's weekday posts
       before the weekend, while the Saturday docket report, the Sunday Senate
       report and the Tuesday Chiefs report still land on the Monday side. 21:00
       is clear of every other job: the daily SITREP writes at 06:00 and the
       watchdog checks at 07:00, 12:00, 18:00 and 22:00.

       StartInterval 96 hours was weighed as the alternative on 2026-10-10 and
       rejected, and the reason is measured rather than assumed. An interval's
       phase belongs to the loaded job, not to the file: a 15-second probe agent
       fired at +15s and +30s, and after bootout/bootstrap the next fires came
       at +17s and +32s, one full interval after the reload instead of where the
       old phase would have put them. So this plist could state an integer and
       not a time, no test could pin the schedule, and the reinstall that
       follows every plist edit would re-anchor the clock. 96 hours is also
       exactly four days, so the weekday advances by four and a Friday fire
       lands once per four weeks.

       Weekly rather than per-commit on purpose: the deterministic gates run in
       CI on every push, but the expensive checks (fetch every cited URL, verify
       every quotation against its own source) need network and are far too slow
       for the deploy path. Scheduled, they still catch rot in content nobody has
       touched in months, which is where every defect found on 2026-09-19 had
       been sitting. -->
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>Weekday</key>
      <integer>1</integer>
      <key>Hour</key>
      <integer>14</integer>
      <key>Minute</key>
      <integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>Weekday</key>
      <integer>5</integer>
      <key>Hour</key>
      <integer>21</integer>
      <key>Minute</key>
      <integer>0</integer>
    </dict>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/prh/Developer/huffmanwrites</string>
  <!-- Keep the exported PATH used by the other huffmanwrites jobs so hugo,
       python3 and the keychain-backed helpers resolve the same way. -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>/Users/prh</string>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/prh/Library/Logs/weekly-integrity.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/prh/Library/Logs/weekly-integrity.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
